course: everyday
lesson: 210
title: First aid, medicine and the sun
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Small injuries need simple, correct care: a cut is cleaned and covered, a bruise gets something cold, and a burn goes under cool running water for 20 minutes.
explain: Some things must always be told to an adult, even when you feel fine: a hard bump on the head, a burn, sunburn.
explain: Medicine is chosen by adults and doctors: aspirin is not given to children under 16 unless a doctor says so, because it can cause a rare but serious illness called Reye's syndrome.
explain: A boy in Arizona touches a hot oven door; he holds his hand under cool running water for 20 minutes and calls his dad, because cooling stops the burn going deeper.
explain: The sun can burn skin even on a nice day, so sunscreen of SPF 30 or more, broad-spectrum, goes on about 20 minutes before going out.
fact: The body needs water to work well: about six to eight cups a day, more when it is hot or you are active.
fact: Aspirin should not be given to children under 16 unless a doctor says, because it can cause Reye's syndrome.
fact: In hospital you can ask questions and tell the nurses how you feel; they are there to help you get better.
fact: A small cut is washed with clean water, pressed with a clean cloth to stop bleeding, and covered with a plaster or bandage.
fact: Cleaning a cut stops infection, and covering it keeps dirt out.
fact: A bruise gets a cold pack wrapped in a cloth for a few minutes, because cold reduces swelling and pain.
fact: After a hard bump on the head you tell an adult even if you feel fine, because a head injury needs watching.
fact: A burn is cooled under cool running water for 20 minutes and an adult is told, because cooling stops the burn going deeper.
fact: With hay fever, you tell a parent, keep windows closed on high-pollen days and wash your face after being outside.
fact: Sunscreen of SPF 30 or more, broad-spectrum, goes on about 20 minutes before going out in summer sun.
fact: Sunburn damages skin and raises the risk of skin cancer later.
fact: If you get sunburnt, you get out of the sun, cool the skin with a cool shower, drink water and tell an adult.
exercise: You are thirsty after football. What is the best drink? | Water | A fizzy drink | An energy drink | A milkshake
exercise: About how much water does a child need a day? | About six to eight cups, more when hot or active | One small glass | Twenty cups | None, food is enough
exercise: Your mum thinks of giving you aspirin for a headache. What is true? | Aspirin is not for children under 16 unless a doctor says | Aspirin is the best and safest medicine for children of every age | Children need double the adult dose | Any medicine in the cupboard is fine to take
exercise: You are in hospital and feel worse. What do you do? | Tell the nurses how you feel and ask questions | Stay quiet so that you do not bother the busy nurses | Wait until you go home to say | Try to fix it yourself
exercise: You scrape your knee in the playground. What is the right first aid? | Wash it with clean water, press with a clean cloth, then cover it | Leave it open with the dirt in | Rub mud on it to stop the bleeding | Put butter on it
exercise: Why do you cover a clean cut with a plaster? | To keep dirt out | To make it heal into a scar | To keep it nice and warm | So nobody can see it
exercise: You bump your arm on a table and a bruise appears. What helps? | A cold pack wrapped in a cloth for a few minutes | A hot water bottle straight away | Rubbing it hard | Ice put straight on the bare skin for a whole hour
exercise: You hit your head hard on the climbing frame but feel fine. What do you do? | Tell an adult anyway | Keep playing and tell nobody | Go to sleep straight away | Climb again to show you are fine
exercise: Why should an adult know about a hard bump on the head? | A head injury needs watching | So they can tell you off for it | So they can fix the climbing frame | Bumps on the head always need a plaster
exercise: You touch a hot iron. What do you do? | Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult | Put butter or toothpaste on it | Put ice straight on it | Pop any blisters
exercise: Why is a burn cooled for so long? | Cooling stops the burn going deeper | It washes all the germs out of the skin | It is really only there to stop you crying | Cold water makes new skin grow back quickly
exercise: It is spring and you sneeze with itchy eyes every day. What helps? | Tell a parent, keep windows shut on high-pollen days, wash your face after being out | Open all the windows for fresh air | Roll in the grass to get used to it | Rub your eyes hard
exercise: When do you put on sunscreen? | About 20 minutes before going out | Only after you get red | Only when it is cloudy | Once a year at the start of summer
exercise: Which sunscreen is best? | SPF 30 or more, broad-spectrum | SPF 5, as it feels lighter on the skin | Any baby oil | Sunscreen from years ago
exercise: You got sunburnt at the beach. What do you do? | Get out of the sun, take a cool shower, drink water and tell an adult | Stay in the sun to turn it brown | Put on a hot towel | Scratch the burnt skin off
test: A girl in Brisbane plays tennis on a hot day and drinks almost nothing. What may happen? | Her body may not work well, as it needs more water when hot and active | Nothing, the heat gives her energy | She will need less water in heat | She will get stronger from thirst
test: Your older cousin is 14 and has a headache. Why not just give him aspirin? | Under 16, aspirin can cause a rare but serious illness | Aspirin only works for grown-ups who are over 60 years old | Aspirin is a sweet with no effect | Teenagers never really get proper headaches
test: A boy cuts his finger on paper and it bleeds a little. What should he do first? | Wash it, press a clean cloth on it, then cover it | Wipe it on his trousers and carry on | Put it in his mouth for an hour | Leave it uncovered so the air can dry it out quickly
test: Which is true about bruises? | Cold reduces swelling and pain | Heat makes them go away at once | Bruises only ever happen to adults | A bruise always needs a plaster on it
test: After falling off her scooter and hitting her head, a girl says she is fine and wants to keep playing. What should happen? | An adult should be told and keep watching her | She should keep riding as fast as before, to shake it off | She should hide the fall from her parents | She should take aspirin
test: A boy in Ireland spills hot tea on his hand. How long should it be under cool running water? | About 20 minutes | About 5 seconds only | About 1 minute or so | No water, just a cloth
test: A girl in Perth puts sunscreen on only when she arrives at the beach and starts swimming. What would be better? | Put it on about 20 minutes before going out | Put it on after swimming, when her skin is dry again | Use none, since she is in the water | Use it only on her face
test: Why does sunscreen matter for children even if they do not mind going red? | Sunburn raises the risk of skin cancer later | Red skin is only a colour change and soon fades | Sunburn makes you taller | It only matters for adults
test: A boy with hay fever plays outside on a high-pollen day. What should he do when he comes in? | Wash his face and keep the windows closed | Open all the windows | Rub his eyes hard | Lie down on the lawn
test: In hospital a girl is scared and does not understand what will happen. What helps? | Asking the nurses and doctors questions | Pretending she understands, so nobody worries | Hiding under the bed | Saying nothing until she goes home
test: burnt my hand on the pan ow!! | Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult | Put butter on it | Put ice straight on it for an hour | Pop the blister and carry on
test: hit my head at football but im fine now | Tell an adult anyway, a head injury needs watching | Don't tell, you're fine | Just sleep it off alone | Keep heading the ball
test: going to the beach, sunscreen when we get there? | Put it on about 20 minutes before going out | Only once you start to feel hot on the sand | Only when you're already red | No need if it's cloudy
test: kid's got a headache, give him aspirin? hes 9 | No, not under 16 unless a doctor says | Yes, aspirin's the normal one for children | Yes, half an aspirin | Yes, with orange juice
explain: A girl in Queensland spent a whole day at the beach without sunscreen or a hat; her skin blistered, she had a fever, and she could not sleep for the pain.
explain: A toddler in the USA found his grandmother's pills in her handbag and swallowed some thinking they were sweets; he was rushed to hospital and had to stay overnight.
fact: Sunburn in childhood raises the risk of skin cancer later in life.
fact: Medicines are a leading cause of poisoning in young children, so all pills must be kept up and away and given only by an adult.
test: found some pills on the floor, they look like sweets | Don't eat them, give them to an adult | Taste just one tiny one to check what they are | Give them to your little brother to try | Put them back in the bag they came from
test: Why does Amy's mum make her wear a hat and sunscreen at noon? | Strong sun burns skin and raises the cancer risk later | Sunscreen makes you tan faster | Hats keep your hair clean | It's only for grown-ups
test: mate cut his hand, bleeding loads | Press a clean cloth on it hard and get an adult | Wash it in the sea, the salt water will clean it out | Let it bleed for a bit to clean it out | Put a plaster on and carry on
test: my aunty swears butter on a burn is best, her friend's kid burnt his hand on the oven and they did that and it was fine. real lesson? | Cool running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult | Butter cools a burn much faster than cold water ever could | Ice straight on the burn is best | Leave the burn alone in the air
test: did u hear a boy bumped his head at footy, said he was fine, then passed out at home and was in hospital for a week. what's the real lesson? | Tell an adult after any hard head bump, even if fine | Only tell someone if there's blood coming out of the bump | Head bumps only matter for grown-ups | Sleep it off straight away
test: apparently that girl got sunburnt so bad on holiday her skin all peeled off in one go like a snake lol. what's probably blown up? | Peeling in one go; but sunburn really harms skin | That sunburn hurts | That sunburn makes skin peel | That sun can burn skin
exam: Identify the correct treatment of a burn. | Cooling under cool running water for twenty minutes | The application of butter to the skin | The application of ice directly to the skin for an hour | Covering with a woollen cloth at once
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the administration of aspirin to children under sixteen? | It ought not to be given unless a doctor so directs | It is the preferred remedy for fever in all young children | It may be given in any quantity | It is suitable for babies only
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning sunburn sustained in childhood. | It increases the risk of skin cancer in later life | It confers lasting protection against sunburn in later years | It is harmless provided it heals | It affects only persons with dark skin
exam: Ascertain the proper course of action following a hard blow to the head, the child feeling well. | An adult is to be informed, as the injury requires observation | Nothing whatever is required where the child feels perfectly well | The child is to run in order to clear his head | The child is to sleep at once and tell nobody
